elaine: FYI customer (acme) opened a support case — streaming response is being cut mid-JSON. Parser error: "Unexpected end of JSON input". They captured the last chunk which ends like: '..., \"items\": [{ \"id\": 42, \"text\": \"This is a partial sentence' then connection closes. Request id: rwd-req-7b2f9.

olivia: Can you drop the minimal curl/node repro and region? Also confirm whether they're hitting our hosted endpoint or a proxy in front.

elaine: Hosted (api.redwood.ai), region us-east-1. Customer sent this curl they used to repro:

elaine: ```bash
curl -v -N \\  -H "Authorization: Bearer <REDACTED>" \\  -H "Accept: text/event-stream" \\  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\  -d '{"model":"rwd-13b-infer","stream":true,"input":"..."}' \\  https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate/stream
```

marco: they say it happens ~1/10 runs. sometimes full JSON arrives after retries. network shows a FIN from a proxy in the middle of the stream.

ashok: ran the server logs for rwd-req-7b2f9 — edge proxy returned 502 at 2025-11-13T14:21:12Z with message: "upstream connection closed while sending response". No application-level error. Looks like an intermediate TCP/TLS FIN before the final JSON chunk.

ashok: hypothesis: intermediate proxy (customer infra or CDN) is enforcing an idle/stream size policy and issuing an early FIN on chunked responses. When that happens the client sees truncated JSON.

olivia: do we have a short repro request we can run locally to validate?

ashok: yes, try this Node snippet (streams raw chunks and prints boundaries):

ashok: ```js
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
(async () => {
  const res = await fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate/stream', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Authorization': 'Bearer <KEY>', 'Accept': 'text/event-stream', 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ model: 'rwd-13b-infer', stream: true, input: 'repeat long output to force multiple chunks' })
  });
  res.body.on('data', chunk => {
    console.log('CHUNK-START');
    console.log(chunk.toString());
    console.log('CHUNK-END');
  });
})();
```

ashok: Running that behind a local nginx reverse-proxy with default proxy_buffering reproduced the truncation within ~5 runs. Disabling buffering (`proxy_buffering off;`) fixed it.

elaine: customer uses an enterprise proxy appliance that buffers and appears to cut long-lived chunked streams. They can try proxy config change but asked if we can provide a client-side workaround.

ashok: short-term mitigation options: 1) ask client to set `Connection: close` so the request is not multiplexed/reused by middleboxes, 2) switch to non-buffered SSE headers (we already send `text/event-stream`), 3) add small heartbeat bytes in the payload to keep buffers active, or 4) fall back to non-streaming (polling full result) for critical calls.

olivia: can you add a small server-side safeguard to ensure final chunk includes a small checksum trailer? if not feasible quickly, we should document the proxy compatibility note in Support KB.

ashok: adding a checksum trailer server-side touches compatibility; tracking: ENG-RUNTIME-437. For now, I recommended they try `Connection: close` and test with curl `--no-buffer` + `-H 'Connection: close'` — that reproduced success for them.

elaine: customer confirmed `Connection: close` + `--no-buffer` avoided truncation in 3/3 runs. They want a formal note for their infra team to change proxy rules.

olivia: created ticket INC-2025-1129 and assigned to eng-runtime; adding chair/priority P2. @ashok please follow up with a short KB blurb and a suggested nginx snippet to disable buffering.

ashok: nginx snippet to share:

ashok: ```nginx
location / {
  proxy_http_version 1.1;
  proxy_buffering off;
  proxy_request_buffering off;
  chunked_transfer_encoding on;
}
```

eng-bot: Incident INC-2025-1129 created by olivia and assigned to eng-runtime.

elaine: adding customer contact (marco@acme) and next steps: provide KB + reproduction logs, mark as monitored until two weeks of successful runs. :white_check_mark:

olivia: tagging for visibility: :eyes: — will escalate to enterprise CSM if they need config changes on our side.
